A||||16||DecadenceR||Hard drive help?????? PLEASE????||||1||
Z||000000||DecadenceR||08-10-2001||01:47 PM||||Okay. I just put in a new 40 gig 7200 rpm hard drive. I used drive copy to move all the info from the old 13 gig failing drive. I started up the computer and... YAY!! IT WORKS!!!!<BR>But....<BR>I went into My Computer, and it says my hard drive is....<BR>Only 13gigs!!!!!<BR>Now I know that I can just re-format my hard drive, re-install windows, and everythign should be fine. I DO NOT WANT TO DO THAT. <BR>I have 7.8 gigs of music files alone, not to mention the 3 gigs of movies that i just got on there recently.<BR>Does anyone know of a program or somethign i can run to fix this?<BR>I ran Norton Utilites 2001 and no dice. The Bios does pick up that it's a 40 gig, so this seems to be just a Windows problem.<BR>TIA to anyone that can help. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||p4n1c||08-10-2001||01:51 PM||||Drive copy makes a partition the size of your original layout of the old drive.  So if it's 13 gigs it makes a 13 gig partition.<P>Just create some new partitions and use the rest of your drive.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||08-10-2001||02:05 PM||||Can't you use something like Partition Magic a make one partition without losing anything?||||reg||1
Z||000003||p4n1c||08-10-2001||02:31 PM||||I really wouldn't want one 40 gig partition let alone 13, but to each their own.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||08-10-2001||03:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>Can't you use something like Partition Magic a make one partition without losing anything?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That was going to be my suggestion, as well.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||DecadenceR||08-10-2001||04:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>Drive copy makes a partition the size of your original layout of the old drive.  So if it's 13 gigs it makes a 13 gig partition.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>But if it just made a partition, wouldn't it also show the other partition as a second drive? because it doesn't shw anything else at all. Just my one drive.||||reg||1
Z||000006||My7Fieros||08-10-2001||05:27 PM||||Hmmm...odd that it doesnt show it automatically in "my computer" as an E drive (or F,G,H...you know). Usually when you add hardware, the comp will automatically recognize it and "label" it per say.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Leadfoot||08-10-2001||05:29 PM||||Did you install the bios overlay?  So older computers do not support large hard disks.  I forget but I think it went in steps of like 2.2G, 8G, 13.5G.....or something like that.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-10-2001||05:43 PM||||partition magic tends to "hide" drives you never named.. you wouldnt have had a chance to name the 40 's partitions..<P>run partition magic and remove or rename the entended partitions..<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||maryjane||08-10-2001||05:57 PM||||I can't remember exactly where I went in my change to larger hd, but remember having to change from "normal" to "enable large block addressing"(lba). If your os allows fdisk, look at the partitions, there is a check box or question to answer whether you want the partition to include entire hd. Partition magic is much better than fdisk tho.||||reg||1
Z||000010||p4n1c||08-10-2001||06:24 PM||||No, programs don't automatically create partitions(well maybe some programs do, but they make me shudder).  Drive Copy creates a partition the size of your old drive and copies everything into it.  Hence, drive copy.<P>You must create the remaining partitions(using fdisk or you can use Partition Magic) and format them for Windows.<P>[edit]As well, it has nothing to do with overlays, BIOS or anything else.  It's a simple matter of a new clean drive, that he used a drive copying program(the name is deceiving, it's actually a partition copier) on and it created a nice 13 gig partition on the clean drive to put the old drive contents in, leaving the other 27 gigs as unpartitioned/formatted for the OS.[/edit]<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 08-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Songman||08-10-2001||06:33 PM||||I'm not gonna say BIOS is the problem, but I am going to say it CAN BE the problem...<P>This happened to me just a month ago working on a laptop. I knew there was a 3gig HD in this machine but no matter what I did it would never find it larger than 120megs. Running fdisk, I deleted, created, and formatted over and over. Finally, I went in to the BIOS and there was a selection for the HD. I changed it to AUTO and went back to fdisk and it worked just fine.<P>Personally, I can't stand a several partitions looking like a D, E, F, whatever drives... My C Drive is all one drive. My D Drive is all one drive. I do all of mine for the maximum amount of the drive. It's just more comfortable for me to work that way.<P>I agree about using Partition Magic.. after you check the BIOS.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||08-11-2001||04:15 AM||||The old drive size cap is 2 gigs.||||reg||6
Z||000013||FieroSTETZ||08-11-2001||04:27 AM||||almost any drive limitation can be fixed with a drive overlay though, got a gb maxtor to work in my 386 freebsd machine. it was... odd...||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cliff Pennock||08-11-2001||08:00 AM||||Check the manufacturer's website of either the old or the new drive. They usually have a utility for download that will copy the contents of the old drive onto the new drive.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Jake_Dragon||08-11-2001||08:52 AM||||<A HREF="http://support.microsoft.com/support/kb/articles/Q255/8/67.ASP?LN=EN-US&SD=gn&FR=0&qry=fdisk&rnk=11&src=DHCS_MSPSS_gn_SRCH&SPR=W98SE" TARGET=_blank>Q255867 - How to Use the Fdisk Tool and the Format Tool to Partition or Repartition a Hard Disk</A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||DecadenceR||08-11-2001||05:52 PM||||Well, i did the Fdisk thing, and it worked. Which will be easier because all I am going to use the second partition for are my audio and movie files anyway, so it'll be easier for my scan disk and defrag if it sees 2 smaller drives. Thanks a lot for yuor help guys.||||reg||1
